## RESOLVED AND REMOVED (ledger, 2026-10-06)

- **"No congruence relation, so `≡ (mod n)` is spelled as ℤ_n equality"** — WRONG when
  written, removed after probing. Congruence IS representable and the bridge DOES exist:
  `aata/3.1-integers-mod-n.md` encodes `a ≡ b (mod n)` directly as `divides(n, sub(a, b))`
  on ℤ (and says so explicitly), and `std/integer/mod-n.b4m:59` has
  `axiom clsCongruent: forall a, b: Int; cls(a) = cls(b) iff divides(N, isub(a, b))`.
  So RSA's `x^{DE} ≡ x (mod n)` is statable as Judson writes it; §7.2 just stops at
  `power(g, …) = g` in the group. Connecting them is a short unwritten proof, not a
  missing primitive. The wrong guess is logged because it was cheap to make and would
  have sent someone building a predicate that already exists in another spelling.

